Our approach is inspired by earlier RLHF methodologies \cite{Yang2023D3PO} and incorporates contemporary advancements in efficient parameter adaptation. By integrating these ideas, QD\textsuperscript{2}P offers a novel and cost-effective technique for fine-tuning high-dimensional generative models under human supervision.

\subsection{Concluding Remarks}
The move from reward-based training to direct incorporation of human preferences in diffusion models represents an important evolution in generative modeling. By employing a lightweight Q-probe in the latent space, our method captures quality improvements driven by human feedback while mitigating the computational challenges associated with full backpropagation through the denoising process. The theoretical foundations and algorithmic details presented here lay the groundwork for the subsequent experimental evaluations, which demonstrate the superior efficiency and performance of QD\textsuperscript{2}P relative to traditional approaches.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}
\subsection{Overview of Q-Denoising Diffusion Probe}

The proposed Q-Denoising Diffusion Probe (QD\textsuperscript{2}P) method integrates human feedback into diffusion model fine-tuning by leveraging an embedding-space probe. Building on the principles of Direct Preference for Denoising Diffusion Policy Optimization (D3PO) and the Q-Probe framework, QD\textsuperscript{2}P mitigates the computational and memory overhead incurred by full-model gradient updates. In our approach, the pre-trained diffusion model remains frozen, while a lightweight linear Q-probe is trained to score intermediate latent embeddings produced during the denoising process. At each denoising step, the fixed diffusion model generates multiple candidate latent states via different noise samples. The Q-probe subsequently evaluates these candidates, and a softmax reweighting of the quality scores determines the updated latent state. This dual-stage guidance adaptively steers the diffusion trajectory toward outputs that are more closely aligned with human preferences.

\noindent The principal advantages of QD\textsuperscript{2}P include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dual-Stage Guidance:} A two-step mechanism where the frozen diffusion model proposes multiple latent updates and a lightweight Q-probe evaluates each candidate. The candidate with the highest softmax weight is selected for the next denoising step.
    \item \textbf{Lightweight Architecture:} By limiting training to a low-parameter Q-probe, the framework significantly reduces computational load and memory consumption relative to full-gradient updates on the entire model.
    \item \textbf{Embedded Feedback Loop:} The Q-probe is updated using either direct human feedback or proxy quality measures, effectively learning an implicit mapping that approximates an optimal reward function without requiring a complex reward model.
    \item \textbf{Efficiency via Distillation:} An optional distillation step allows the Q-probe corrections to be absorbed into the diffusion model via low-rank adaptation, significantly reducing inference overhead by obviating the need for repeated candidate evaluations.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Technical Formulation}

Let $x_t$ denote the latent state at denoising step $t$, and let $f(\cdot)$ represent the fixed diffusion transformation used to generate candidate updates. At each step, QD\textsuperscript{2}P generates $k$ candidate latent states as follows:
\[
  x_{t+1}^{(i)} = f(x_t, \eta_{t}^{(i)}), \quad \text{for } i = 1, \dots, k,
\]
where $\eta_{t}^{(i)}$ is the noise sample for candidate $i$ at step $t$. A linear Q-probe, denoted by $Q(\cdot)$, assigns a quality score to each candidate:
\[
  q^{(i)} = Q\bigl(x_{t+1}^{(i)}\bigr), \quad i = 1, \dots, k.
\]
These scores are converted into weights via a softmax function with temperature $\tau$:
\[
  w^{(i)} = \frac{\exp\Bigl(\frac{q^{(i)}}{\tau}\Bigr)}{\sum_{j=1}^{k}\exp\Bigl(\frac{q^{(j)}}{\tau}\Bigr)} \quad \text{for } i = 1, \dots, k.
\]
The updated latent state is computed as the weighted sum of the candidate proposals:
\[
  x_{t+1} = \sum_{i=1}^{k} w^{(i)} \; x_{t+1}^{(i)}.
\]
This process is recursively applied over successive denoising steps until the final latent state is reached.

\subsection{Algorithmic Description}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:QD2P} summarizes the QD\textsuperscript{2}P procedure. In this algorithm, the frozen diffusion model produces candidate latent states, the Q-probe evaluates each candidate, and the softmax reweighting aggregates these candidates to form the new latent state.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{QD\textsuperscript{2}P: Q-Denoising Diffusion Probe}
\label{alg:QD2P}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Input:} Initial latent state $x_0$, diffusion model $f(\cdot)$, Q-probe $Q(\cdot)$, candidate count $k$, total steps $T$, temperature $\tau$.
\For{$t \gets 0$ \textbf{to} $T-1$}
    \State \textbf{Candidate Generation:}
    \For{$i \gets 1$ \textbf{to} $k$}
        \State Sample noise $\eta_{t}^{(i)}$.
        \State Compute candidate $x_{t+1}^{(i)} = f(x_t, \eta_{t}^{(i)})$.
    \EndFor
    \State \textbf{Quality Evaluation:}
    \For{$i \gets 1$ \textbf{to} $k$}
        \State Compute quality score $q^{(i)} = Q\bigl(x_{t+1}^{(i)}\bigr)$.
    \EndFor
    \State \textbf{Reweighting:}
    \State Compute
    \[
       w^{(i)} = \frac{\exp\Bigl(\frac{q^{(i)}}{\tau}\Bigr)}{\sum_{j=1}^{k}\exp\Bigl(\frac{q^{(j)}}{\tau}\Bigr)} \quad \text{for } i = 1, \dots, k.
    \]
    \State \textbf{Aggregation:} Set
    \[
      x_{t+1} \gets \sum_{i=1}^{k} w^{(i)}\, x_{t+1}^{(i)}.
    \]
    \State Update $x_t \gets x_{t+1}$.
\EndFor
\State \textbf{Output:} Final latent state $x_T$.
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Implementation Details}

In our implementation, the diffusion model is pre-trained and frozen during QD\textsuperscript{2}P updates. The only trainable component is the Q-probe, defined as a simple linear layer. Its training objective minimizes the mean squared error between its evaluations and a target quality signal derived from proxy human feedback:
\[
  \min_{\theta_Q} \; \mathbb{E}\Bigl[\| Q\bigl(x_{t+1}\bigr) - y_{t+1} \|_2^2\Bigr],
\]
where $\theta_Q$ denotes the parameters of the Q-probe and $y_{t+1}$ is the target quality signal at step $t+1$.

To reduce inference overhead, an optional distillation step is introduced. In this step, the Q-probe corrections are absorbed into a lightweight adapter module via low-rank adaptation. A linear correction is applied to the latent state as follows:
\[
  \tilde{x}_{t+1} = x_{t+1} + x_{t+1} \; A,
\]
where $A$ is a small parameter matrix learned through distillation to mimic the corrective effect of the Q-probe.

\subsection{Summary of Contributions}

Our method contributes to the field in several key aspects:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Efficient Dual-Stage Guidance:} QD\textsuperscript{2}P decouples candidate generation from quality evaluation, enabling adaptive reweighting in latent space without the computational expense of full-model gradient updates.
    \item \textbf{Lightweight Q-Probe Integration:} Employing a simple, trainable linear layer for scoring latent candidates eliminates the need for a complex reward model and drastically reduces GPU memory usage.
    \item \textbf{Modular and Interpretable Framework:} The transparent candidate reweighting mechanism, governed by a softmax function, offers a tunable strategy for incorporating human feedback into the generation process.
    \item \textbf{Optional Distillation for Inference Efficiency:} By distilling the Q-probe corrections into the diffusion model via low-rank adaptation, significant reductions in inference time are achieved with negligible loss in generation quality.
\end{itemize}

Overall, QD\textsuperscript{2}P provides a robust and resource-efficient framework for fine-tuning diffusion models, effectively combining the benefits of frozen diffusion pipelines with direct human-aligned optimization.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental}
%% Experimental Setup

\subsection{Synthetic Diffusion Evaluation Protocol}
We fine-tune pretrained diffusion models by reformulating the denoising process as a multi-step Markov Decision Process (MDP). Two pipelines are compared: a baseline that performs full-gradient updates using a Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) style loss, and our proposed Q-Denoising Diffusion Probe (QD\textsuperscript{2}P) method. In Q-Denoising Diffusion Probe, the main diffusion network is kept frozen and only a lightweight linear Q-probe is trained on intermediate latent embeddings. At every denoising step, QD\textsuperscript{2}P generates $k$ candidate latent proposals. A softmax over the Q-probe outputs computes weights used to aggregate the candidate proposals into the final selected latent vector. Generation quality is quantified by a proxy quality metric defined as the cosine similarity between an output latent vector $x$ and a predefined ideal latent vector $x^{ideal}$:
\[
Q(x,x^{ideal}) = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \frac{x_i \cdot x^{ideal}_i}{\|x_i\|\,\|x^{ideal}_i\|},
\]
where $N$ is the batch size. In parallel, computational cost is tracked using Python timing utilities.

The overall synthetic evaluation procedure is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:synthetic}.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Input:} Pretrained diffusion model $\mathcal{D}$, Q-probe $Q$ with latent dimension $d$, batch size $N$, total denoising steps $T$
\State Set the ideal latent vector $x^{ideal} = \mathbf{1}_{1\times d}$
\State Initialize latent state $x \sim \mathcal{N}(0,I)$
\For{$\text{step} = 1,2,\ldots,T$}
    \State Set noise level $\eta = 1/\text{step}$
    \State \textbf{// Baseline update}
    \State Compute $x_{baseline} = \mathcal{D}(x,\eta)$
    \State Evaluate quality $Q_{baseline} = Q(x_{baseline}, x^{ideal})$
    \State \textbf{// QD\textsuperscript{2}P update}
    \For{$j = 1,2,\ldots,k$}
        \State Generate candidate $c_j = \mathcal{D}(x,\eta)$
        \State Compute Q-probe score $q_j = Q(c_j)$
    \EndFor
    \State Stack candidates $C = [c_1,\ldots,c_k]$ and scores $q = [q_1,\ldots,q_k]$
    \State Compute weights $w = \mathrm{softmax}(q)$
    \State Compute final candidate $x_{QD^2P} = \sum_{j=1}^{k} w_j\,c_j$
    \State Evaluate quality $Q_{QD^2P} = Q(x_{QD^2P}, x^{ideal})$
    \State Update Q-probe parameters using an MSE loss on the aggregated Q-values
    \State Set $x \leftarrow x_{baseline}$ \Comment{Alternatively, one can choose $x_{QD^2P}$}
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\caption{Controlled Quality Evaluation Procedure}
\label{alg:synthetic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Ablation Study on the Candidate Reweighting Mechanism}
To isolate the impact of the candidate reweighting mechanism, we vary both the number of candidate proposals $k$ and the softmax temperature $T$ used during reweighting. For each configuration $(k,T)$, the same diffusion model and Q-probe are employed. The proxy quality metric is tracked over the denoising steps to assess convergence speed and stability. In addition, detailed logs of average Q-values and the indices of the selected candidates are recorded to facilitate subsequent analysis.

\subsection{Efficiency Benchmarking and Distillation Strategy}
We assess the resource efficiency of QD\textsuperscript{2}P by comparing the full pipeline with a distilled variant. In the full QD\textsuperscript{2}P approach, candidate reweighting mechanism is applied at every diffusion step. In the distilled version, after the diffusion update a lightweight adapter module is applied to absorb the Q-probe corrections. In particular, the distilled latent is computed as
\[
x_{distilled} = x_{diffused} + x_{diffused} \cdot A, \quad A \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d},
\]
where $d$ is the latent dimension. Total inference time is measured using Python timing functions, and GPU memory consumption is monitored using appropriate \texttt{torch.cuda} utilities. The cosine similarity metric (as defined above) is used to confirm that the distilled output maintains generation quality comparable to the full QD\textsuperscript{2}P approach.

\subsection{Parameter Settings and Contributions}
The experiments are conducted with the following parameter settings:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Latent Dimension}: 16
    \item \textbf{Batch Size}: 32
    \item \textbf{Denoising Steps}: 10
    \item \textbf{Candidate Count}: 5 (for the full QD\textsuperscript{2}P setting)
    \item \textbf{Softmax Temperature}: 1.0 by default (varied in the ablation study)
\end{itemize}

The key contributions of our experimental method are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dual-Stage Guidance}: Multiple candidate latent updates are generated at each step, and a lightweight Q-probe is used to select the most promising update, effectively bridging sampling and optimization.
    \item \textbf{Light-weight Efficiency}: By freezing the main diffusion model and updating only a low-parameter Q-probe, our method substantially reduces computational and memory overhead compared to full-gradient fine-tuning strategies.
    \item \textbf{Embedded Feedback Loop}: The continual update of the Q-probe with proxy quality metrics embeds a feedback mechanism directly in the latent space, enabling adaptive and stable reward correction.
    \item \textbf{Smooth Reweighting}: The softmax-based candidate reweighting yields a smooth, KL-constrained update that enhances stability without compromising output quality.
\end{itemize}

This experimental design enables comprehensive evaluation of QD\textsuperscript{2}P with respect to generation quality, parameter sensitivity, and computational efficiency. Detailed quantitative results and corresponding figures are presented in the Results section.

